#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367395 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f59392c4-996f-5b29-9c37-7a1b75ffd896" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Outward-Pointing Unit Normal Vector for a Positively Oriented Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b66d3403-d1ff-5652-97ef-b5fc07e9083a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367397} {:answer/id #uuid "9b02fc59-0ee5-5f88-aa22-a383ea9ef6a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367398} {:answer/id #uuid "649eb34d-7fa3-5df2-a42d-7cf87bca86b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367399} {:answer/id #uuid "cbe30193-140b-5cab-9364-627cb4b6776d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367400} {:answer/id #uuid "4fe029fb-17de-5063-8724-d9623056cb9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367401}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b02fc59-0ee5-5f88-aa22-a383ea9ef6a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367398} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a9a599e-0887-532e-b219-a52806bdc8ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367396}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6dc83e63-c6ff-5ed8-99ed-5a528dba71ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-126915" :question/problem "The closed, positively oriented curve $C$ is defined as $r(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{a(t)\\,i+b(t)\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $b(t).$" :question/worked-solution "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $b(t)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367402 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f59392c4-996f-5b29-9c37-7a1b75ffd896" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Outward-Pointing Unit Normal Vector for a Positively Oriented Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "edc8a2ab-9d67-55d5-9581-57269cfea2aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367404} {:answer/id #uuid "89a7e83d-52a7-5da1-861b-3c8170465cb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}-x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367405} {:answer/id #uuid "365ccbc9-7971-578f-9a76-251ecd88d3ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4x \\\\ 4y\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367406} {:answer/id #uuid "fb22d257-820d-5ede-b92d-0c8eeff56850" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367407} {:answer/id #uuid "c1a5ec29-e85a-52b5-bba2-a4509f38b698" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}y \\\\ -x\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367408}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "edc8a2ab-9d67-55d5-9581-57269cfea2aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367404} :answer-field/id #uuid "7365734d-b574-543d-a51d-344c06ffd158" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367403}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "568e518b-6dee-5582-961d-6548648b3418" :question/math-academy-id "q-126918" :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16.$ Find the outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C.$" :question/worked-solution "We can parameterize $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi )\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation).\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n||{r}^{′}(t)||=\\sqrt{(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}=4,\n$$\n\nand since for our curve $x(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y(t)=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ it follows that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367409 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad522514-328e-5944-b4c7-91d7e3b302f0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Outward-Pointing Unit Normal Vector for a Negatively Oriented Curve"}] :question/a