11\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -11\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -11\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}11\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}11\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{11\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $a(t)=11\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367416 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad522514-328e-5944-b4c7-91d7e3b302f0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Outward-Pointing Unit Normal Vector for a Negatively Oriented Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb994a79-fb4e-51c7-8daa-7ce7499d5829" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\operatorname